  "prompt_text": "The figure illustrates vibrational normal modes in a one-dimensional diatomic chain made of alternating masses, labeled \\(M_1\\) and \\(M_2\\), connected like a spring-mass lattice. It contrasts optical and acoustical modes for both longitudinal motion along the chain and transverse motion perpendicular to it, while indicating wave propagation with the wavevector \\(k\\).\n\nThe key visual message is the phase relation between neighboring atoms. In the optical modes, adjacent large and small masses move in opposite directions, so the two sublattices oscillate against each other. This is shown both for longitudinal motion, where arrows point oppositely on neighboring atoms, and for transverse motion, where the two masses lie on opposite sides of the sinusoidal displacement pattern. In the acoustical modes, neighboring atoms move together in the same direction, giving an in-phase collective motion of the chain. For the transverse acoustic case, both masses follow the same wave-like shape.\n\nOverall, the diagram emphasizes how a diatomic lattice supports two distinct families of vibrations because of the alternating masses: acoustical modes with in-phase motion and optical modes with out-of-phase motion, each possible for different polarization directions.",
